   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let f be a function with a continuous derivative such that \\( f(1) = 2 \\), \\( f(3) = 5 \\), and \\( \\int_{1}^{3} f(x) \\, dx = 4 \\). Find the value of \\( \\int_{1}^{3} x \\, f'(x) \\, dx \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 9
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = x \\) and \\( dv = f'(x) \\, dx \\), so \\( du = dx \\) and \\( v = f(x) \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then the integral equals \\( \\left[x \\, f(x)\\right]_{1}^{3} - \\int_{1}^{3} f(x) \\, dx \\), and the boundary term is \\( 3(5) - 1(2) = 13 \\).",
         "mathjson": 13
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The remaining integral is given: \\( \\int_{1}^{3} f(x) \\, dx = 4 \\).",
         "mathjson": 4
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So the value is \\( 13 - \\left(4\\right) = 9 \\).",
         "mathjson": 9
      }
   ],
